oving  new oil fields in the uk which is  ridiculous and also its tax regime  actually is hugely beneficial for these  oil companies that actually means that  the uk in the north sea is the most  profitable country in the world to  extract oil and gas which is completely  like not compatible with any sort of  climate leadership so i would be kind of  cautious and to make out that boy  johnson is a great climate leader um  given all of that  let's come back to the weakened  political capital of of the pm if we may  there was a quote in that report from  steve baker conservative mp he says i  genuinely believe that when the full  costs of net zero start hitting us if  people have never been given a choice at  the ballot box we could end up with  something bigger than the poll tax  certainly bigger than brexit because the  numbers of people hit by it and their  inability to cope will be  huge is there a democratic deficit with  all of this push to net zero tom would  you say  no there's not and uh steve baker is  one out of touch with what most people  and we've seen enormous amount of  evidence both directly and what people  do and also uh in polling that people  want to get on with dealing with climate  change but also this is a man who is  opposed to green levees but on uh monday  he voted in favor of a green level we  haven't got and actually don't need so  in order to support nuclear power so i'm  not sure i'm going to use steve baker as  an indicator of what the british public  are going to uh want to do  okay your opinion coming through pretty  loud and clear there tom michaela we've  got a pandemic to pay for we were still  paying for the financial crisis and now  we need to pay for net zero do we just  need a mindset shift that the days of  the uk as being a relatively low tax  country need to change  i think when we're talking about how  much money we have for things like to  pay for the pandemic or everything else  we need to also remember that the uk  government has paid huge amounts of  public money to oil and gas companies so  the same companies that are polluting so  much and the same companies that are  paying out their shareholders in  billions so since signing the paris  agreement um the uk government has paid  400 billion pounds of public money to um  shell alone and shell in that same time  as paid 45 billion to their shareholders  so actually there's a huge amount of  public money that's going in the wrong  direction it's promoting the polluters  and if we instead redirected those funds  towards a just transition towards  funding net zero so it's not being paid  for by ordinary people but instead um by  our like our funds that are meant for  all of us and by and the companies that  are actually causing the most harm so  the companies that are causing harm  shouldn't be um being paid out loads of  money and in public payments they should  be taxed um rightfully and fairly and  because they should be the ones funding  this transition not ordinary people a  trust transition tom do you think that  is anywhere on the government's agenda  i'm not sure it's on the government's  agenda at the moment i was just going to  say where uh michaela is absolutely  right if the government uh had not  abandoned green levees in uh the uh last  uh in the cameron years we'd now be uh  consumers in this country now be saying  about two and a half billion less than  they're currently paying so it really is  as michaela says it's a question of who  pays uh for this and right now there's  an obvious answer if you want to help  with the immediate short term problem  caused by the grass prices then what you  could do is tax some of those uh very  large profits additional profits that  are going to the uh oil and gas  companies and use that to increase  universal benefit as we did during the  lockdown to those people who are most  disadvantaged and create a real peaceful  just transition but that's a thing that  we'll have to see whethe